> What's a fairly new effect (of significant impact just yesterday), is
> that NH command keys are ineffetive, occasionally. (Don't think so, but
> could the number of game observers influence the resposiveness?)

Nah, shouldn't. When a game starts a completely separate fork gets a
copy of the tty and writes it to disk. Viewers are basically running
ttyplay on a file that is just being created in realtime. It has
nothing to do with the game itself anymore, as the game is in a
completely separate thread in control of the actual tty (rather than
the copy or the file that is written).

> Hangup's occasionally appeared while watching games. A random thought
> (which might be completely wrong, of course) was that some tty color
> or tty positioning control sequences might disturb my xterm.

That is entirely possible, although I'd expect it to happen more often
if so, since there are a finite number of these that are used.

> I've no idea though, and not done any investigation. Is there anything
> I can do client-side-wise to locate the source of the problem(s)?

We'd probably need a strace of the process just before it hung to see
what characters got sent across, and if the hang is just a wait for I/
O (it probably is). We'd probably also need to match it up against the
dgamelaunch process watching the game to see what state that process
was in. Since it is hung, not disconnected, I suspect that the OS at
least believes the tcp session to be alive.

When watching you said these are hangs, not disconnects? If so, then
your tcp session is still believed to be alive, but you aren't getting
data.

Could be a bug in the part of dgamelaunch that plays back those
inprogress tty files to your term. (something caused by some transient
condition) Probably tough to track down. I'm open to ideas (although I
probably don't have time to stick gdb sessions on running dgamelaunch
sessions to look for it). I wonder if other folks see similar
behavior, and how widespread this is. If it is a dgamelaunch problem,
then others should be seeing it too.

